  "id": "Compound::DB00517 - Gene::1131",
  "verbalization": "Anisotropine Methylbromide is likely to interact with Chrm3 through a mechanism involving muscarinic acetylcholine receptors. The evidence suggests that Anisotropine Methylbromide binds to Chrm1, which is a component of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors. These receptors are known to mediate responses to acetylcholine, a neurotransmitter that plays a key role in various physiological processes. The fact that Chrm1 participates in the activity of muscarinic acetylcholine receptors, which in turn are involved in the participation of Chrm3, indicates a potential pathway where Anisotropine Methylbromide may influence Chrm3 through its interaction with Chrm1 and the broader muscarinic receptor system. This relationship is supported by the consistent binding of Anisotropine Methylbromide to Chrm1 across different paths, suggesting a robust and well-defined interaction. The evidence also highlights the importance of G-protein coupled receptor activity in linking these interactions, reinforcing the idea that Anisotropine Methylbromide may modulate Chrm3 through a receptor-mediated mechanism.",
